deal because it's part of the meld. \n\nIf I discard T9, then I can still have one T9 left. Maybe that's better for forming a pair. Let's see. If I discard T9, the remaining T9 can be part of a pair. Let's check the total tiles. After discarding T9, my hand would be [W4, T1, T7, T8, T9, B1, J1x2, J2x2]. Wait, no, I had two T9s. If I discard one, I have one left. Then, the melds would be [T5, T6, T7] and maybe another Chi if I can form T7-T8-T9. But if I have T7 (from hand), T8, and T9, that's a Chi. So if I discard T9, I can't form that Chi anymore. Hmm. \n\nAlternatively, if I keep T9 and use it in the Chi, then discard another tile. Let's think about the scoring patterns. If I can form multiple Chis and pairs, maybe that's worth points. For example, if I have two Chis and a pair, that could be part of a Full Flush (all same suit) if all are in the same suit. But my current melds are in T (Bamboo) and I have other tiles in different suits. Wait, my hand includes W4 (Characters), T1, T7, T8, T9 (Bamboo), B1 (Dots), J1, J2 (Dragons). So not a Full Flush. \n\nAnother possibility is to form a Seven Pairs. But I have J1x2, J2x2, T9x2, and others. But I don't have seven pairs. \n\nLooking at the scoring patterns, maybe Outside Hand is possible. Outside Hand requires that every meld and pair has terminals or honors. My current melds are [T5, T6, T7] (which are non-terminal, so not part of Outside Hand). So that's a problem. If I can restructure to have all melds and pair with terminals or honors. \n\nAlternatively, if I can form a Pung of J1 or J2. Since I have two each, I need one more. But if I discard a J1 or J2, someone might take it. \n\nWait, the valid actions include Play J1 and Play J2. If I discard J1, maybe someone can Peng it. Similarly for J2. If I discard a J1, there's a risk of losing the chance to form a Pung. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard T1, which is a terminal. Since I have T1 in hand, maybe keeping it for a pair or meld. \n\nLet me think about the cur